---
/**
 * What the website collects, and what the application does not.
 *
 * Kept short and specific. A privacy page that lists categories of data
 * "such as" other data is a page that has decided not to tell you.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO } from '../site';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — Sukhi Play"
  description="The Sukhi Play application sends nothing anywhere. The website counts visits with Google Analytics, but only if you agree first."
  page="privacy">

  <main class="wrap doc" style="padding: 44px 0 80px;">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="doc-summary">Two different things: the application you install,
      and this website. They behave differently, and it is worth being exact
      about which is which.</p>

    <div class="doc-body">
      <h2>The application</h2>
      <p><strong>It sends nothing anywhere.</strong> There is no account, no
        sign-in, no analytics, no telemetry and no crash reporting. Nothing
        about what your child does leaves the computer. The list of sites, the
        play-time limit and every setting live in a file on your own disk.</p>
      <p>It makes exactly one request of its own accord: when you press
        <em>Check for updates</em>, it asks GitHub whether a newer release
        exists. Nothing is sent with that request beyond what any HTTP request
        carries, and it happens only when you press the button.</p>
      <p>Websites you add are ordinary websites. What they collect is between
        you and them, which is why every entry in the catalogue links to that
        site&rsquo;s own terms.</p>

      <h2 id="analytics">This website</h2>
      <p>This site uses <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong> to count visits
        and see which pages get read. How it starts depends on where you are,
        because the law does:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>In the EU, the EEA, the UK and Switzerland</strong> it does
          not start at all until you say yes. Until then nothing is loaded from
          Google &mdash; not the script, not a cookie, not a request. The UK
          gained a statistical-purposes exemption in February 2026, but it does
          not cover Google Analytics, because that sends the data to a third
          party.</li>
        <li><strong>Everywhere else</strong> it starts on, a notice says so
          once, and you can turn it off in a click. Turning it off also deletes
          the cookies it had already set.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Your region is worked out from your browser&rsquo;s own time zone.
        There is no geo-IP lookup: asking a third party where you live, in
        order to decide how carefully to treat your privacy, would be an odd
        way to begin.</p>
      <p>If you have <strong>Do Not Track</strong> or <strong>Global Privacy
        Control</strong> switched on, nothing loads anywhere in the world,
        whatever the local rules permit.</p>
      <p>When it is on, it records the usual: a page address, a rough location
        from a truncated IP, a browser and device type, and which site sent you
        here. Advertising features and ad personalisation are switched off, so
        nothing is used to build a profile or target anything at you.</p>
      <p>Your answer is remembered in your own browser, in
        <code>localStorage</code>, not in a cookie sent to anyone.
        <a href="#analytics" data-consent-reopen>Change your mind</a> whenever you like.</p>

      <h2>Hosting</h2>
      <p>The site is static files served by GitHub Pages, and downloads come
        from GitHub Releases. GitHub keeps its own server logs of requests to
        both, which this project does not control and cannot see. Their
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
           rel="nofollow noopener">privacy statement</a> covers that.</p>

      <h2>Asking</h2>
      <p>If something here is unclear or wrong, <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open
        an issue</a> and it gets fixed on this page.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
